[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch0-the-idea"]
[ChapterTitle "The idea"]
[DefaultTags "development"]

{The Alapin answers the Sicilian with 2.c3, and the whole opening is in that one pawn move: White means to play d4 next with a pawn behind it, so that when Black takes on d4 White takes back with a pawn and keeps a centre. The open Sicilian trades that centre away; the Alapin keeps it, and plays the chess the London and the Colle already taught you, reached by 1.e4.

## What 2.c3 gives up

Two things. The knight's best square is c3 and the pawn is standing on it, so the queen's knight comes out later. And the move does nothing for development, which is why Black's two best replies both hit the e-pawn at once: 2...Nf6 with a piece and 2...d5 with a pawn. Everything else on move two is easier for White, and the later chapters say how.

## The two structures

Against 2...Nf6 White pushes 3.e5, the knight goes to d5, and White gets a wedge on e5 with a big centre behind it. The knight on d5 is Black's best piece and White spends the opening asking it to move; every square it goes to is worse than the one it left. Chapter 1 is that fight.

Against 2...d5 White takes, the queen comes to d5, and after d4 and ...cxd4 and cxd4 White has an isolated d-pawn. That is the other structure, and a fair trade: the pawn is a weakness in the ending and a strength in the middlegame, with open lines behind it, an outpost on e5, and the d5 break waiting for the moment Black's pieces are not looking. Chapter 2 is that fight.

## Piece homes

The king's knight goes to f3 and the bishop to d3 or c4, whichever hits something. The queen's knight comes to c3 once the c-pawn has gone to d4, or to d2 when it has not. The bishop on c1 goes to e3 in the isolated-pawn positions and to f4 or g5 in the wedge ones. The king castles short early, because nearly every line opens the centre.

## The Vienna and the Alapin

Same first move, opposite temperament. The Vienna gives a pawn on move three to get the f-file and the centre by force. The Alapin gives nothing and takes the centre with pawns, a move at a time. The Grand Prix Attack, 2.Nc3 and 3.f4, is the Vienna's cousin against the Sicilian and was the other candidate; this course chose the structural answer, because that is what your White courses are.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Taking back on d4 with the knight when the pawn was the point. Playing d4 before the c-pawn can take back, which hands Black the open Sicilian he wanted. Letting the knight sit on d5 in the wedge lines instead of asking it to move. And in the isolated-pawn lines, trading pieces because they are there to trade: every trade makes the pawn weaker and the plan behind it smaller.}
1. e4
{The first move of your two 1.e4 courses. The Vienna answers 1...e5; this course answers 1...c5.}
1... c5
{The Sicilian, the most common reply to 1.e4 at club level. Black trades the fight for the centre for a fight on the wings, and the open Sicilian gives him what he wants. [%weight 8]}
2. c3
{The Alapin. The pawn is going to d4 next, and when Black takes there the c-pawn takes back, so White keeps a pawn centre rather than trading it away. [%tags big-centre] [%cal Gc3d4]}
( 2. Nf3!?
{The open Sicilian, and a whole library with it. This course exists to avoid that library.}
) ( 2. Nc3!?
{The Grand Prix Attack when f4 follows, which is the Vienna's cousin. Sound, and the other temperament; the course chose the structural answer.}
) ( 2. d4?!
{Too early: 2...cxd4 and there is no pawn to take back with. 3.c3 dxc3 is the Smith-Morra, a gambit, and 3.Qxd4 Nc6 is a tempo given away.}
) 2... Nf6
{The main reply. The knight hits e4 while the c-pawn cannot defend it, and White has to push. [%weight 6]}
3. e5
{The wedge. The pawn goes forward with tempo and it will be the centre of everything: it takes f6 and d6 from Black's pieces and it is the pawn the whole structure stands behind. [%tags e5-wedge] [%cal Ge4e5]}
( 3. d3?!
{It defends the pawn and gives up the whole idea: with the d-pawn on d3, d4 never comes with a pawn behind it.}
) ( 3. Bd3?!
{The bishop defends e4 and blocks its own d-pawn. 3...Nc6 and Black is ahead in development against a centre that is not coming.}
) 3... Nd5
{The knight goes to its best square. From d5 it watches c3 and f4, and it is the piece White spends the next ten moves asking to move. [%weight 9]}
4. d4
{The centre, with the c-pawn ready to take back. This is the move 2.c3 was for. [%tags big-centre]}
( 4. Nf3!? {Fine, and it usually transposes. The pawn first, because it is the point.} ) (
4. c4?!
{It chases the knight before the centre exists: 4...Nc7 and the c-pawn has given up d4 for good.}
) 4... cxd4
{Black takes, which is what he came to do. Now the recapture question: with the pawn or the knight. [%weight 8]}
5. Nf3
{Develop first and take back on the next move. The knight has to come here anyway, and taking with the pawn at once lets 5...Nc6 hit d4 before it has a defender.}
( 5. cxd4!?
{Also fine and it usually transposes; the knight first means the pawn on d4 is defended the moment it arrives.}
) ( 5. Qxd4?!
{The queen takes and 5...e6 6.Nf3 Nc6 hits it: a tempo given away, and the pawn centre that was the point is not there.}
) 5... Nc6
{The main move, hitting d4 a second time before White has taken back. [%weight 7]} 6. cxd4
{Now the pawn takes back, with the knight on f3 already defending it. White has the centre the opening promised. [%tags big-centre]}
( 6. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and the centre is gone: 6...Nxd4 7.cxd4 is a pawn on d4 with no c-pawn beside it, and 6...Nxe5 simply takes the wedge.}
) ( 6. Bc4?! {6...Nb6 hits the bishop and d4 is still hanging with the c-pawn undecided.}
) 6... d6
{Black hits the wedge at once, which is the right plan: a wedge nobody attacks is a wedge that wins. [%weight 8]}
7. Bc4
{The bishop hits the knight, and the knight has to decide. Every square it can go to is worse than d5.}
( 7. exd6?!
{It trades the wedge for nothing: 7...Qxd6 and Black's pieces are all on good squares with the centre open.}
) ( 7. Nc3!?
{Sound, and 7...Nxc3 8.bxc3 leaves a doubled pawn. The bishop asks the question first.} )
7... Nb6 {The knight hits the bishop back, which is its only active choice. [%weight 8]}
8. Bb5
{The bishop pins the knight to the king, so that d6 cannot take on e5 without the c6 knight being taken. [%tags tactics]}
( 8. Bb3!?
{Sound and quieter: 8...dxe5 9.d5 gets the pawn back with play. The pin keeps the wedge standing.}
) ( 8. Bd3?! {8...dxe5 9.dxe5 Nb4 and the bishop is chased off the diagonal it just took.}
) 8... dxe5 {Black takes the wedge, which was going to happen. [%weight 8]} 9. Nxe5
{The knight takes back and the pin now bites: c6 is attacked twice and Black has to deal with it. [%tags tactics]}
( 9. dxe5?!
{9...Qxd1+ 10.Kxd1 and the queens are off with White's king in the middle and the pin gone.}
) 9... Bd7 {Black breaks the pin by offering the bishops. [%weight 8]} 10. Nxd7
{Take, and take with the knight rather than the bishop: the bishop on b5 stays to keep c6 pinned for one more move.}
( 10. Bxc6!?
{Also fine, and it hands over the bishop pair for nothing when the knight could take.} ) (
10. Nxc6?!
{10...Bxc6 11.Bxc6+ bxc6 and Black has a bishop against a knight with the d-file to himself.}
) 10... Qxd7 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 11. Nc3
{The queen's knight at last, to the square the c-pawn left. It covers d5 and e4 and it is ready for d5 itself.}
( 11. O-O!? {Fine. The knight first, because d5 is the square the game is about.} )
11... e6 {Black covers d5 and gets ready to castle. [%weight 8]} 12. O-O
{Castle. The position is level and White has what the opening promised: a pawn on d4, a knight on c3 beside it, and the freer pieces. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Put the rooks on d1 and e1, the bishop on b5 back to d3 when the knight on c6 moves, and play d5 when Black's pieces are not looking at it. If Black plays ...Nxd4 the pawn is not lost: Qxd4 and the knight on c3 covers everything.] [%line The shape: the wedge, the pin and d4]}
( 12. d5?!
{The break before the king is safe: 12...exd5 13.Nxd5 Nxd5 14.Qxd5 Qxd5 and White is simply a pawn down, because the knight on c6 was defended all along and nothing wins it back.}
) ( 12. Qb3!?
{Sound, hitting b7. The king first; the queen comes out once nothing can hit it back.} ) *


[CourseId "alapin-sicilian"]
[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch1-nf6-the-wedge"]
[ChapterTitle "2...Nf6: the e5 wedge"]
[DefaultTags "vs-nf6"]

{This is the main line, and the one a prepared opponent plays. 2...Nf6 hits e4, the pawn goes to e5 with tempo, and the knight lands on d5, the best square it will ever have. Everything White does for the next ten moves is about that knight: hit it with the bishop, hit it with the c-pawn where the c-pawn is free, and make it choose between b6, where it hits the bishop and does nothing else, and a retreat.

## The shape

3.e5 Nd5 4.d4 cxd4 5.Nf3, and only now the recapture on d4. The knight comes first because it defends the pawn that is about to arrive, and because 5.cxd4 Nc6 would hit d4 a move before it had a defender. Black's three replies at move five are 5...Nc6, 5...e6 and 5...d6, and this chapter has a recipe for each. The main one, 5...Nc6 6.cxd4 d6 7.Bc4 Nb6 8.Bb5, is chapter 0's line run further: the bishop pins the knight, e5 is taken and taken back, the bishops come off on d7, and White castles into a level position with the freer pieces.

## The recapture question

Take back on d4 with the pawn every time the pawn can do it. The knight on d4 is what the open Sicilian gets, and this course was chosen to get something else. The one time the pawn cannot take back, after 5...e6 6.cxd4 b6 and a trade on c3, the b-pawn takes back and White has a pawn on c3 beside the one on d4: a different centre, still a centre.

## What the wedge does

The pawn on e5 takes f6 and d6 from Black's knights, so the king's knight has nowhere natural to go once it leaves d5, and the bishop on f8 has to come out by e7. When Black plays ...d6 to hit the pawn, the pin on b5 makes ...dxe5 cost the knight on c6, and when Black plays ...e6 first the pawn stands for good. Do not defend it with pieces; let it be taken when taking it costs Black something.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Taking back on d4 with the knight. Playing c4 to hit the knight on d5 before d4 is in, which gives up the centre for one tempo. Letting the pin on b5 go by trading on c6 when the knight could take on d7 instead. And castling long, or not castling at all, in a position where the centre is about to open.}
1. e4 c5 2. c3 Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6
{The main move. Black hits d4 a second time before White has taken back, so the pawn recapture has to come now. [%weight 7]}
( 5... e6
{The second main move. Black covers the knight first and keeps ...Nc6 and ...d6 for later. [%weight 4]}
6. cxd4 {The pawn takes back, defended by the knight. [%tags big-centre]} ( 6. Bc4!?
{Fine: 6...Nb6 7.Bb3 and the pawn takes back next. The pawn first leaves nothing hanging.}
) ( 6. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and the centre is a pawn on e5 with nothing beside it: 6...Nc6 7.Nxc6 bxc6 and Black has the centre pawns.}
) 6... b6
{The bishop is coming to b7, where it looks at the knight's square and the long diagonal. [%weight 5]}
( 6... d6 {Black hits the wedge with ...e6 already in. [%weight 5]} 7. Bc4
{The bishop hits the knight, as always. [%tags development]} ( 7. exd6?!
{7...Bxd6 and Black is a move from castling with the wedge gone.} ) 7... Nb6
{The knight hits back. [%weight 8]} 8. Bb3
{Back to b3, keeping the diagonal, because there is no knight on c6 to pin. [%tags development]}
( 8. Bb5+!?
{Fine: 8...Bd7 9.Bd3 and the bishop has spent two moves to go where it could go in one.} )
( 8. Bd3!? {Sound. b3 keeps the pressure on e6 and f7 for the day the centre opens.} )
8... Nc6 {Black develops and hits d4 and e5 at once. [%weight 7]} 9. exd6
{Now the wedge trades itself, because with the knight on c6 and the pawn on e6 there is no pin to hold it and d4 needs the file. [%tags big-centre]}
( 9. O-O!?
{Sound: 9...dxe5 10.dxe5 Qxd1 11.Rxd1 and the ending is level. Taking first keeps the queens on.}
) ( 9. Nc3?!
{9...dxe5 10.dxe5 Qxd1+ 11.Nxd1 and the knight has taken back on the wrong square.} )
9... Qxd6 {The queen takes back and stands in the centre. [%weight 6]} 10. O-O
{Castle, with an isolated d-pawn and the pieces to play behind it. This is chapter 2's structure reached from chapter 1. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Nc3, Re1 and Be3 behind the pawn, with Ne4 hitting the queen on d6 when the moment comes. The d5 break is the plan, and Black's queen on d6 is in its way.] [%line 5...e6 and 6...d6, the isolated pawn]}
( 10. Nc3!?
{Also fine. The king first, because ...Nxd4 tricks appear once the knight leaves c3's cover of d4 behind.}
) ) 7. Nc3
{The knight hits the knight. With the bishop coming to b7 to defend d5, the trade on c3 is the moment to force. [%tags development]}
( 7. Bd3!? {Sound and slower: 7...Bb7 and the knight on d5 is defended twice.} ) (
7. Bc4!? {Fine: 7...Bb7 8.O-O and the knight sits on d5 defended by the bishop.} )
7... Nxc3 {Black trades. [%weight 8]} 8. bxc3
{The b-pawn takes back, and White has pawns on c3, d4 and e5: a centre with a doubled pawn in it, and the b-file half open for a rook. [%tags big-centre]}
( 8. Qd2?
{8...Nd5 and the knight is back on its square, hitting c3 with the queen the only thing holding it; White loses material at once.}
) 8... Qc7 {The queen hits the wedge and the pawn on c3 behind it. [%weight 7]} 9. Bd2
{The bishop defends c3 without blocking anything, and it is heading for d3 to point at h7. [%tags development]}
( 9. Qd2!? {Fine, and the queen is not where it wants to be. The bishop does the job.} ) (
9. Bd3?
{9...Qxc3+ 10.Qd2 Qxa1 and the rook has gone: the check picks up c3 and a1 together.} )
9... Bb7 {Black develops the bishop to the long diagonal. [%weight 8]} 10. Bd3
{The bishop points at h7, and with the wedge on e5 keeping Black's knight off f6, that diagonal is the attack. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan Castle, Qe2, and Ng5 with the queen behind it when Black's knight is not on f6. The pawn on c3 is a target and c4 is the answer to ...Nc6, closing the diagonal on b7.] [%line 5...e6 and 6...b6, the b7 bishop]}
( 10. Be2!? {Sound and passive. The diagonal to h7 is what the wedge was for.} ) (
10. c4?!
{It fixes the pawns before the pieces are out, and 10...Nc6 hits d4 with the bishop on b7 looking through.}
) ) ( 5... d6 {Black hits the wedge before anything else. [%weight 3]} 6. cxd4
{The pawn takes back, defended by the knight. [%tags big-centre]} ( 6. Bc4!?
{Fine: 6...Nb6 7.Bb3 dxe5 8.Nxe5 and the pawn is still to be taken back on d4.} ) (
6. exd6?! {6...Qxd6 and the wedge is gone for nothing, with the queen standing well.} )
6... Nc6 {Black develops with a hit on d4 and e5. [%weight 7]} ( 6... dxe5
{Black takes the wedge at once, before the knight on c6 can be pinned. [%weight 4]}
7. Nxe5
{The knight takes back, and with no knight on c6 the pawn on d4 is not even attacked: Black's own knight on d5 stands in the queen's way. [%tags development]}
( 7. dxe5?!
{The pawn takes back and the knight on f3 has to guard it for the rest of the game: 7...e6 and Black develops against a wedge with nothing behind it.}
) 7... e6 {Black covers the knight and opens the bishop. [%weight 7]} 8. Bc4
{The bishop hits the knight, which has nothing to hide behind now. [%tags development]} (
8. Bd3!? {Sound, and it does not ask the knight anything.} ) 8... Nd7
{Black offers a trade of knights to ease the pressure. [%weight 6]} 9. O-O
{Castle, and let Black be the one to trade on e5: the knight there is defended, and a queen on g4 comes next against a king that has not castled. [%tags king-safety]}
( 9. Nxd7?!
{9...Bxd7 and the bishop takes back to a good square with the tension gone; the knight on e5 was worth more kept.}
) ( 9. Bxd5!?
{Sound: 9...exd5 10.Nd3 and White has traded the good bishop for a knight. The king first.}
) ( 9. Qg4!? {Sound, and 9...N7f6 hits it. The king first, then the queen.} ) ( 9. Bb3!?
{Fine: 9...Bb4+ 10.Nd2 O-O and the bishop has retreated for nothing.} ) 9... Be7
{Black develops. [%weight 8]} 10. Qg4
{The queen to g4 against the king in the middle, hitting g7 and eyeing e6, with the knight on e5 behind it. [%tags kingside-attack]}
( 10. Nc3!?
{Sound: 10...Nxc3 11.bxc3 and the pawns on c3 and d4 are a centre. The queen first, while the king has not castled.}
) 10... O-O {Black castles. [%weight 8]} 11. Re1
{The rook behind the knight on e5, so that ...Nxe5 can be met by dxe5 with the rook holding it, and the e-file is White's. [%tags development] [%plan Nc3 to hit the knight on d5, Bd3 when it moves, and Bh6 with the queen on g4 when the bishop on e7 has gone to f6. The knight on e5 is the piece Black has to trade, and trading it costs a move.] [%line 5...d6 and 6...dxe5, the early trade]}
( 11. Nc3!?
{Sound: 11...Nxc3 12.bxc3 and the same shape with the pawns doubled. The rook first keeps the knight in the centre.}
) ) 7. Bc4
{The bishop hits the knight and the position is the main line by another order: 7...Nb6 8.Bb5 is chapter 0. [%tags development] [%plan Chapter 0's line from here: 7...Nb6 8.Bb5 dxe5 9.Nxe5 Bd7 10.Nxd7 Qxd7 11.Nc3 e6 12.O-O.] [%line 5...d6, into the main line]}
) 6. cxd4
{The pawn takes back with the knight on f3 defending it. The centre the opening promised. [%tags big-centre]}
6... d6 {The main move, hitting the wedge at once. [%weight 7]} ( 6... e6
{Black covers d5 before hitting the wedge. [%weight 3]} 7. Bc4
{The bishop hits the knight anyway; ...e6 only means that ...Nb6 is the reply rather than a choice. [%tags development]}
( 7. Bd3!?
{Sound, and the bishop on c4 asks the knight a question that the bishop on d3 does not.} )
( 7. Nc3!?
{Fine: 7...Nxc3 8.bxc3 and the doubled pawn is a centre pawn. The bishop first keeps the knight on the board.}
) 7... Nb6 {The knight hits the bishop. [%weight 8]} 8. Bb5
{The pin again, and with the pawn on e6 there is no ...d6 to take the wedge with tempo. [%tags tactics]}
( 8. Bb3!?
{Sound. The pin does more: it holds the knight on c6 while White castles and brings the other knight to c3.}
) ( 8. Bd3?! {8...Nb4 hits the bishop and the pawn on d4 is short of a defender.} )
8... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 9. O-O {Castle. [%tags king-safety]} ( 9. Nc3!?
{Also fine. The king first, because ...d6 is coming and the centre opens with it.} )
9... O-O {Black castles. [%weight 8]} 10. Nc3
{The knight to c3, covering d5 and e4, and White has the whole setup with a wedge Black has not yet touched. [%tags development] [%plan Re1 behind the wedge and Bd3 when the knight on c6 moves. Black's break is ...d6 or ...f6; meet ...d6 with exd6 only when Bxd6 can be hit by Ne4, and meet ...f6 with exf6 and the e-file.] [%line 6...e6, the wedge stands]}
( 10. Bxc6?!
{10...dxc6 and Black has the bishop pair and the d-file, and the wedge has no target to hold.}
) ( 10. Re1!? {Sound. The knight first, because d5 is the square the game is about.} ) )
7. Bc4 {The bishop hits the knight, which has to decide. [%tags development]} 7... Nb6
{The knight hits back, its only active choice. [%weight 7]} ( 7... e6
{Black covers the knight rather than moving it, and gets ready to castle. [%weight 4]}
8. O-O
{Castle, with the bishop already on the best diagonal. The knight on d5 is left standing; it is answered by the queen next move. [%tags king-safety]}
( 8. Bxd5?!
{It trades the good bishop for the knight and 8...exd5 gives Black the centre pawns and the bishop pair.}
) ( 8. exd6?! {8...Bxd6 and Black has traded the wedge away for free development.} )
8... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 9. Qe2
{The queen comes behind the e-pawn, so that ...dxe5 can be met by dxe5 with the queen defending it, and the d-file is left for a rook. [%tags development]}
( 9. exd6?! {9...Qxd6 and Black is developed with the wedge gone.} ) ( 9. Nc3!?
{Sound: 9...Nxc3 10.bxc3 and the pawns on c3 and d4 are a centre still. The queen move keeps the knight on the board to be chased.}
) 9... O-O {Black castles. [%weight 8]} 10. Rd1
{The rook behind the d-pawn, so that d4 is defended when the knight leaves d5 and the d-file is White's if it opens. [%tags development] [%plan Nc3 to hit the knight, Bd3 if it leaves d5, and Qe4 pointing at h7 when the bishop is on d3. If Black takes on e5, take back with the pawn and the e-pawn is a wedge again with a rook behind d4.] [%line 7...e6, the queen behind the wedge]}
( 10. Nc3!?
{Also fine. The rook first, because the knight can go to c3 or d2 depending on what Black does with d6.}
) ( 10. Bd2!? {Quiet and sound. The rook is the piece with a job on the d-file.} ) )
8. Bb5 {The pin, so that ...dxe5 costs the knight on c6. [%tags tactics]} 8... dxe5
{Black takes the wedge. [%weight 7]} ( 8... Bd7
{Black breaks the pin before taking on e5. [%weight 3]} 9. exd6
{Trade the wedge now, while the knight on c6 is still pinned: the d-file opens with Black's bishop on d7 in the way of his own queen, and the bishop on b5 keeps its diagonal for one more move. [%tags big-centre]}
( 9. Bxc6?!
{9...Bxc6 10.exd6 exd6 and Black has the bishop pair and the d-file, with the wedge traded for nothing.}
) ( 9. Nc3!?
{Sound: 9...dxe5 10.dxe5 g6 and the wedge is held by the knight. The trade first opens the file while the pin holds.}
) ( 9. O-O!?
{Sound: 9...Nxe5 10.Nxe5 dxe5 11.Bxd7+ Qxd7 and it is level with the queens on.} ) 9... e6
{Black keeps the bishop on d7 and means to take back on d6 with the bishop from f8. [%weight 7]}
10. O-O
{Castle. The pin holds, the d-file is half open, and the bishop on f8 has to spend a move on d6. [%tags king-safety]}
( 10. Nc3!? {Sound: 10...Bxd6 11.O-O is the same. The king first.} ) ( 10. d5?!
{10...exd5 11.Bxc6 Bxc6 and the bishop pair is Black's with the centre gone.} ) 10... Bxd6
{The bishop takes back. [%weight 8]} 11. Nc3
{The knight to c3, covering d5 and e4, and White has the isolated pawn with the pin on c6 still holding: Black's queen has no square on the d-file while the bishop on d7 blocks it. [%tags development] [%plan Re1, Bg5 and Ne4 against the bishop on d6, with d5 when the knight on c6 has to move. The pawn on d4 is isolated and the pieces behind it are on every open line.] [%line 8...Bd7, the wedge traded]}
( 11. Re1!? {Sound, and the knight first: d5 is the square the game is about.} ) (
11. Bxc6!?
{Sound: 11...Bxc6 12.Nc3 and the bishop pair is Black's. The pin is worth more kept for a move.}
) ) 9. Nxe5 {The knight takes back and c6 is attacked twice. [%tags tactics]} 9... Bd7
{Black breaks the pin by offering the bishops. [%weight 8]} 10. Nxd7
{Take with the knight, so the bishop keeps the pin one more move. [%tags development]}
10... Qxd7 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 11. Nc3
{The queen's knight to the square the c-pawn left. [%tags development]} 11... e6
{Black covers d5 and gets ready to castle. [%weight 8]} 12. O-O
{Castle. Chapter 0 stops here; this chapter goes on. [%tags king-safety]} 12... Be7
{Black develops the last minor piece and is one move from castling. [%weight 8]} 13. Qg4
{The queen comes out to g4 while Black's king is still in the middle. It eyes g7 and e6, and it makes Black castle into it rather than develop first. [%tags kingside-attack]}
( 13. Re1!?
{Sound and quiet: the rook behind the e-file. The queen move asks a question first.} ) (
13. d5?!
{13...exd5 14.Nxd5 Nxd5 15.Qxd5 Qxd5 16.Bxc6+ bxc6 and White is a pawn down for nothing: the knight on c6 was never loose.}
) 13... O-O {Black castles, into the queen's line but out of the centre. [%weight 8]}
14. Bxc6
{Now the trade, because it does two things at once: it doubles Black's pawns on the c-file, and it clears the way for the bishop on c1 to come to h6 with the queen already on g4. The pin has done its work and the bishop has a better job. [%tags kingside-attack]}
( 14. Bd3?
{14...Nxd4 and the pawn has gone: the bishop on b5 was all that kept the knight off d4, and the queen on g4 cannot take back because ...Qxd4 follows.}
) ( 14. Rd1!?
{Sound, holding d4 with the rook. The trade first wins the bishop the tempo it needs.} ) (
14. Be3!? {Sound and it lets 14...f5 push the queen around. The trade first.} ) (
14. Qe4?!
{14...Rac8 15.Rd1 Rfd8 and the queen has moved twice to defend a pawn the trade would have solved.}
) 14... bxc6 {The pawn takes back. [%weight 9]} 15. Bh6
{The bishop to h6 with the queen behind it, hitting g7. Black has to give up the bishop pair or the dark squares round his king, and the pawns on c6 and c7 are targets for the rest of the game. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan Rad1 and Rfe1, then Ne4 heading for c5 or g5. If Black plays ...Bf6, Bg5 trades the piece that holds his king, and the pawns on c6 and c7 are the ending's target.] [%line The main line, 13.Qg4 and 14.Bxc6]}
( 15. Rd1!? {Sound and quiet: the bishop move asks the question the queen went to g4 for.}
) ( 15. Be3!? {Sound and it does nothing the queen on g4 wanted. h6 is the square.} ) *


[CourseId "alapin-sicilian"]
[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch2-d5-the-isolated-pawn"]
[ChapterTitle "2...d5: the isolated pawn"]
[DefaultTags "vs-d5"]

{The other main line. 2...d5 hits e4 with a pawn, White takes, the queen takes back, and after 4.d4 the Alapin's whole structure comes down to one pawn: when Black takes on d4 and White takes back with the c-pawn, the d-pawn stands alone. That is the isolated queen's pawn, and this chapter is how to play with it.

## What the pawn gives

Space and lines. The pawn on d4 takes c5 and e5 from Black's pieces and gives White's knights e5 and, once the c-file is half open, a rook on c1 that looks straight down it. The pieces behind the pawn have open diagonals and open files, and the d5 break, when it comes, opens everything at once. The pawn is weak only when the pieces come off, so the rule of the chapter is the one chapter 0 gave: do not trade because a trade is offered.

## The shape

3.exd5 Qxd5 4.d4 Nf6 5.Nf3, and the bishop on c1 comes to e3 to hold d4 before the knight comes to c3 to hit the queen. That order matters: 6.Be3 first, so that after ...cxd4 and cxd4 the knight comes to c3 with tempo on the queen and the pawn already has a defender; 6.Bd3 cxd4 7.cxd4 Nc6 reaches the same position with a knight on c6 already hitting d4 and the square b4 free for it. Against 5...Bg4 the bishop goes to e2 and h3 asks the question; against 5...Nc6 the same Be3 holds d4 and the knight comes with tempo.

## The recapture question

Take back on d4 with the pawn. The knight recapture gives Black an open Sicilian with the queen already on d5, which is a fine position for Black, and it throws away the one thing this course was chosen for. The isolated pawn is the point, not the price.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing Bd3 before Be3, so that ...cxd4 and ...Nc6 hit d4 before it is held. Taking back on d4 with the knight. Trading the bishop on d3 for a knight on c6 or e4 because the trade is there, which leaves the pawn without its best defender and Black with the bishop pair. And playing d5 as a pawn move rather than a break: it is played when it opens a line onto something, not to get the pawn off the file.}
1. e4 c5 2. c3 d5 3. exd5 Qxd5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 e6
{The main move. Black opens the bishop and keeps the queen's retreat squares. [%weight 7]}
( 5... Bg4
{The pin, before the bishop can be shut in by ...e6. Black's most annoying reply. [%weight 4]}
6. Be2
{Break the pin at once by developing. The bishop on e2 is going nowhere better, and h3 comes next to ask the question. [%tags development]}
( 6. Nbd2!?
{Sound, unpinning by blocking, and the knight is better on c3 once the c-pawn has gone.} )
( 6. Be3?!
{6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Qxf3 8.gxf3 and the queens are off with White's pawns wrecked.} ) 6... e6
{Black opens the other bishop. [%weight 7]} ( 6... cxd4
{Black takes before the bishop is asked anything. [%weight 3]} 7. cxd4
{The pawn takes back. [%tags isolated-pawn]} ( 7. Nxd4?
{7...Qxg2 8.Rf1 and the pawn has gone with the king stuck in the middle: the knight left f3 and the queen walked through.}
) 7... e6 {Black opens the bishop. [%weight 8]} 8. Nc3
{The knight with tempo. [%tags development]} ( 8. h3!?
{Fine: 8...Bh5 9.Nc3 transposes. The knight first takes the tempo while it is there.} )
8... Qa5
{The queen goes to a5, out of the knight's way and pinning it to nothing. [%weight 5]}
9. O-O
{Castle. Nothing is behind the knight on c3, so the pin is empty, and the king leaves the centre before it opens. [%tags king-safety]}
( 9. Qd2?! {9...Bb4 and now the pin is real.} ) ( 9. h3!? {Sound. The king first.} )
9... Nc6 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 10. Be3
{The bishop holds d4, and the position is the main shape with Black's queen on the edge. [%tags development] [%plan h3 to ask the bishop, Qb3 hitting b7 when the bishop leaves the diagonal to d5, and the rooks to c1 and d1. The queen on a5 is hit by Nd2 to c4 or by a3 and b4.] [%line 6...cxd4 and 8...Qa5]}
( 10. d5?!
{10...exd5 11.Nxd5 Nxd5 12.Qxd5 Bxf3 13.Bxf3 Qxd5 14.Bxd5 and it is an ending with nothing in it.}
) ) 7. h3
{Ask the bishop: it retreats to h5 or gives itself up on f3, and either way White knows where it stands before castling. [%tags development]}
( 7. O-O!?
{Fine, and the question on h3 is better asked while the answer still costs Black a move.}
) ( 7. c4?!
{7...Qd8 8.d5 exd5 9.cxd5 and the isolated pawn has become a passed pawn with nothing behind it and Black's pieces around it.}
) 7... Bh5 {The bishop keeps the pin. [%weight 8]} ( 7... Bxf3
{Black gives up the bishop rather than retreat it. [%weight 4]} 8. Bxf3
{The bishop takes back and points down the long diagonal at b7 and the queen on d5. [%tags development]}
( 8. gxf3?
{8...cxd4 9.Qxd4 Qxd4 10.cxd4 Nc6 and White has a wrecked kingside in an ending.} )
8... Qd7 {The queen steps off the diagonal. [%weight 6]} 9. dxc5
{Take the pawn. The queen on d7 cannot take back without walking into the queen trade on d7, and after 9...Bxc5 10.Qxd7+ Nbxd7 11.Bxb7 the pawn on b7 is a pawn: with the queens off and the bishop pair, White is simply a pawn up. [%tags tactics]}
( 9. O-O?!
{9...cxd4 10.cxd4 and the pawn is isolated with nothing won for it, where the pawn on c5 was there to be taken.}
) ( 9. Be3?!
{It holds d4 and 9...Nd5 hits the bishop, while the pawn on c5 that was there to be taken stays on the board.}
) ( 9. Qb3?!
{It hits b7 and 9...Nc6 defends it; the pawn on c5 was the one to take, and it is taken with the queens off.}
) 9... Bxc5 {Black takes back with the bishop. [%weight 8]} 10. Qxd7+
{Trade the queens, so that the bishop on f3 takes on b7 with nothing to punish it. [%tags tactics]}
( 10. Bxb7?
{10...Qxb7 and the bishop has been given for a pawn: the queen on d7 defends b7 until it is traded.}
) ( 10. O-O?! {10...Qc7 defends b7 and the pawn is not coming off. The trade first.} )
10... Nbxd7 {The knight takes back. [%weight 8]} 11. Bxb7
{Take the pawn. The rook comes to b8 and the bishop goes back to a6 or f3, a pawn up with the bishop pair against a Black position that has nothing for it. [%tags tactics] [%plan Ba6 when the rook hits the bishop, Nd2 and O-O-O or Ke2 with the rooks connected, and the a-pawn goes forward with the bishop pair behind it. A pawn up with no weaknesses is the whole plan.] [%line The trade on f3, a pawn on b7]}
( 11. Nd2?! {11...b6 and the pawn on b7 is defended; the bishop was taking it for free.} )
( 11. O-O?! {11...b6 and the pawn on b7 is defended: it was free now and is not later.} )
) 8. O-O
{Castle. The pin is on a bishop now, not a king, and it can be broken with g4 whenever White wants to. [%tags king-safety]}
( 8. g4?! {8...Bg6 and White has weakened the kingside before castling into it.} ) (
8. Be3!? {Sound. The king first, then the bishop.} ) 8... Nc6
{Black develops, hitting d4. [%weight 8]} 9. Be3
{The bishop holds d4 before the knight comes to c3 with tempo. [%tags development]} (
9. Qb3?! {9...Qxb3 10.axb3 and the queens are off with a doubled pawn for nothing.} ) (
9. c4?! {9...Qd6 10.d5 exd5 11.cxd5 Nb4 and the pawn on d5 is a target, not a break.} )
9... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 7]} 10. cxd4
{The pawn takes back: the isolated pawn, with the bishop on e3 behind it. [%tags isolated-pawn]}
( 10. Nxd4?!
{10...Bxe2 11.Qxe2 Nxd4 12.cxd4 and White has traded into a level position with nothing to play for.}
) 10... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 11. Nc3
{The knight with tempo on the queen. [%tags development]} ( 11. Qb3!?
{Sound, and the knight first gains the tempo the queen move would spend.} ) 11... Qd6
{The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 12. Qb3
{The queen hits b7, and with the bishop on h5 the pawn has only the queen to defend it. Black has to spend a move on ...O-O or ...Rb8. [%tags tactics] [%plan Rfd1 and Rac1 behind the pawn, Ne5 hitting the bishop on h5 through the one on e2, and d5 when the knight on c6 moves. If Black plays ...O-O, Qxb7 is not on: the rook on b8 comes next with tempo; take the pawn only when the rook cannot chase the queen.] [%line 5...Bg4, the queen on b7]}
( 12. Ne5!?
{Sound and good, and 12...Bxe2 13.Qxe2 trades the bishop that was annoying White. The queen move asks about b7 first.}
) ( 12. d5?!
{12...exd5 13.Nxd5 Nxd5 14.Qxd5 Bxf3 15.Bxf3 Qxd5 16.Bxd5 and everything has come off for nothing: the break opened no line onto anything.}
) ) ( 5... Nc6 {Black develops the knight before deciding on the bishop. [%weight 3]}
6. Be3
{The bishop holds d4 first, as against 5...e6, so that the knight can come to c3 with tempo. [%tags development]}
( 6. dxc5?!
{6...Qxd1+ 7.Kxd1 e5 and Black has the centre with the queens off and White's king on d1.}
) ( 6. Be2!? {Sound: 6...Bg4 7.h3 is the pin line. The bishop on e3 keeps the pawn first.}
) 6... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 6]} ( 6... Bg4
{The pin, with the bishop on e3 already committed. [%weight 5]} 7. Be2
{Break the pin by developing, as always. [%tags development]} ( 7. Nbd2!?
{Sound, and the knight is better on c3 later.} ) ( 7. h3?!
{7...Bxf3 8.Qxf3 Qxf3 9.gxf3 and the queens are off with White's pawns wrecked: the bishop on e2 first, so that the trade on f3 costs Black the bishop pair and nothing else.}
) 7... e6 {Black opens the bishop. [%weight 8]} 8. h3
{Ask the bishop. [%tags development]} ( 8. O-O!?
{Fine, and the question is better asked first.} ) 8... Bh5
{The bishop keeps the pin. [%weight 8]} 9. O-O
{Castle, and the position is the 5...Bg4 line by another order. [%tags king-safety] [%plan The 5...Bg4 line from here: ...cxd4 cxd4, Nc3 with tempo, and Qb3 against b7.] [%line 5...Nc6 and 6...Bg4, the pin again]}
) 7. cxd4
{The pawn takes back, and the main line is one move away. [%tags isolated-pawn] [%plan Nc3 with tempo, a3, Bd3 and castle: the main line with the bishop on e3 already in place.] [%line 5...Nc6, into the main line]}
( 7. Nxd4?! {7...e5 hits the knight with tempo and the centre is Black's.} ) ) 6. Be3
{The bishop holds d4 before anything hits it, so that Nc3 can come with tempo next move and ...cxd4 never arrives on an undefended pawn. [%tags development]}
( 6. Bd3!?
{Sound: 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 Nc6 8.Nc3 Qd6 is the main line with the bishop already on d3, and ...Nb4 free for Black to hit it.}
) ( 6. Na3!?
{The other theory move, heading for b5 or c4. Sound, and it is more to remember than the bishop.}
) ( 6. dxc5?!
{6...Qxd1+ 7.Kxd1 Bxc5 and the queens are off with White's king in the middle.} )
6... cxd4
{Black takes, which gives White the isolated pawn and the play that comes with it. [%weight 7]}
( 6... Nc6 {Black develops without taking on d4, keeping the tension. [%weight 3]} 7. Bd3
{Develop, with the bishop on e3 already holding d4. The tension is White's to keep: if Black never takes, c4 comes later for space. [%tags development]}
( 7. dxc5!?
{Sound: 7...Qxd1+ 8.Kxd1 and the pawn on c5 is held by the bishop with the queens off. The course keeps the queens on.}
) ( 7. Be2!? {Fine and quieter. d3 is the diagonal the wedge on d4 wants a bishop on.} )
7... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 7]} 8. cxd4 {The pawn takes back. [%tags isolated-pawn]}
( 8. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and 8...Nxd4 9.Bxd4 e5 hits the bishop and the centre is Black's.} ) (
8. Bxd4?! {8...Nxd4 9.cxd4 and the bishop the pawn needed is gone.} ) 8... Qd6
{The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 9. Nc3
{The knight to c3 now that the c-pawn has gone: the main line with the bishop already on d3, so a3 is the next move before ...Nb4 arrives. [%tags development] [%plan a3, castle, and the main line's plan: Qe2, the rooks to the centre files, Ne5 and the d5 break.] [%line 6...Nc6, the tension kept]}
( 9. d5? {9...Nxd5 and the pawn has simply gone: nothing was behind it.} ) ( 9. O-O!?
{Fine: 9...Nb4 10.Be2 and the bishop has been chased once. The knight first, then a3.} ) )
7. cxd4
{The pawn takes back. The d-pawn is isolated now, held by the bishop, with the c-file half open for a rook. [%tags isolated-pawn] [%cal Gd4d5]}
( 7. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and it is an open Sicilian with Black's queen already on d5: a fine game for Black, and not the one the course was chosen for.}
) ( 7. Bxd4?!
{7...Nc6 8.Be3 and the bishop has moved twice to reach the square it could have had in one, with the c-pawn still on c3 and no pawn in the centre at all.}
) 7... Nc6 {Black develops and hits d4 again. [%weight 8]} ( 7... Be7
{Black develops before hitting d4 again. [%weight 4]} 8. Nc3
{The knight with tempo on the queen. [%tags development]} ( 8. Bd3!?
{Fine: 8...O-O 9.O-O and the knight comes next. The tempo on the queen is free now and may not be later.}
) 8... Qd6 {The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 9. Bd3
{The bishop to d3. Without a knight on c6 there is no ...Nb4 to guard against, so a3 can wait. [%tags development]}
( 9. a3!? {Sound and not needed yet: there is no knight on c6 to come to b4.} ) (
9. Bb5+?!
{9...Bd7 10.Bxd7+ Nbxd7 and White has traded the bishop the pawn needs for nothing.} )
9... O-O {Black castles. [%weight 9]} 10. O-O
{Castle, and the position is the main line with Black's knight still on b8. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Qe2, the rooks to d1 and e1, and Ne5 with the bishop on d3 behind it. If the knight comes to c6, a3; if it comes to d7, d5 is closer than it looks.] [%line 7...Be7, the knight still home]}
( 10. Qe2!? {Fine. The king first, as always with the centre open.} ) ) 8. Nc3
{The knight comes with tempo on the queen and covers d5, the break square. [%tags development]}
( 8. Bd3!?
{Fine, and the knight move gains a move on the queen that the bishop move does not.} ) (
8. Be2!? {Sound and slow. The knight hits the queen first.} ) 8... Qd6
{The queen steps to d6, where it keeps an eye on d4 and stays on the file. [%weight 8]} (
8... Qa5
{The queen goes to the edge, pinning the knight on c3 to nothing and hoping for ...Bb4. [%weight 3]}
9. Bd3
{Develop and castle next; the queen on a5 is out of the game and White's pieces are all coming to the centre. [%tags development]}
( 9. a3!?
{Sound, taking b4 away. The bishop first, because the queen on a5 is not going anywhere useful.}
) ( 9. Qd2?! {9...Bb4 and the pin on c3 is real, with the queen on d2 behind it.} )
9... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 10. O-O
{Castle. The pin on c3 is empty: nothing stands behind the knight. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Qe2, Rac1 and Rfd1, then d5 when the knight on c6 moves. The queen on a5 is hit by Nb5 or Bd2 whenever the tempo is wanted.] [%line 8...Qa5, the queen on the edge]}
( 10. Qd2!? {Fine, and it walks into the pin ...Bb4 for no reason. The king first.} ) )
9. a3
{A small move that takes b4 away from Black's knight and bishop, so that Bd3 next is not answered by ...Nb4 chasing it off. [%tags queenside-space]}
( 9. Bd3?!
{9...Nb4 hits the bishop and after it moves ...Nbd5 blocks the pawn on the square White wanted for the break.}
) ( 9. Bc4!?
{Sound: 9...Be7 10.O-O O-O 11.Qe2 and the bishop points at e6. The a-pawn first keeps b4 for White.}
) 9... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 10. Bd3
{The bishop to d3, pointing at h7, with b4 no longer available to a knight. [%tags development]}
( 10. Bc4!?
{Fine. d3 aims at the king and c4 aims at e6; against the setup with ...e6 the king is the better target.}
) ( 10. Bb5?!
{The pin does nothing here: 10...O-O 11.Bxc6 bxc6 gives Black the bishop pair and a solid pawn on c6.}
) 10... O-O {Black castles. [%weight 9]} 11. O-O
{Castle. This is the isolated-pawn position the chapter is about: the pawn held by the bishop, the knights on c3 and f3, the bishop on d3, and every plan still open. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Qe2, Rad1 and Rfe1, then Ne5 or the d5 break when a Black piece has left the centre. The break comes when d5 opens the bishop on d3 or the file for the rook onto something. Never trade the bishop on d3 for the knight on c6.] [%line The main line, 6.Be3 and the IQP]}
( 11. Qe2!?
{Sound, and the king first: the centre is open and Black's queen is on the d-file.} ) (
11. Ne4?!
{11...Nxe4 12.Bxe4 Nxd4 and the pawn has gone while the king is still in the middle.} ) *


[CourseId "alapin-sicilian"]
[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch3-other-second-moves"]
[ChapterTitle "Black's other second moves"]
[DefaultTags "big-centre"]

{2...Nf6 and 2...d5 are the two replies that hit e4 before White has played d4, and they are the two that need a chapter each. Everything else lets White play 3.d4 at once, and this chapter is one recipe for each of the six other moves a club player plays. Most of them reach a shape from chapter 1 or chapter 2 by another road, and the note on each says which.

## The rule

When the e-pawn is not attacked, play d4. Then take back on d4 with the c-pawn, develop the knight to f3 and the bishop to d3 or c4, and castle. Black has spent his second move on something other than the centre, and the centre is the answer to that.

## The six moves

2...e6 is a French player's move and after 3.d4 d5 White takes on d5 and gets the isolated pawn in Black's camp instead of his own. 2...d6 is a slow move, and 3.d4 Nf6 4.Bd3 is the one setup where the bishop goes to d3 before the knight to f3, so that e4 stays defended without e5. 2...g6 lets White build the full centre and meet the fianchetto with Nc3, Be3 and a quick Qd2. 2...Nc6 3.d4 usually turns into chapter 2's pin line with the knight already out. 2...e5 is a King's pawn game where the pawn on c3 is already useful, and 2...b6 is answered by the centre and the queen on e2 holding e4.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing 3.Nf3 out of habit and letting Black have the open Sicilian anyway. Answering 2...e6 with 4.e5, the French Advance, which is a different course. Taking back on d4 with the knight. And against 2...g6, playing d5 before the pieces are out, which gives the bishop on g7 a diagonal for nothing.}
1. e4 c5 2. c3 e6
{A French player's move: the pawn goes to d5 next, and Black wants the French with the pawn on c3 in the way of White's knight. [%weight 6]}
( 2... d6 {A slow move. Black keeps every option and takes none of them. [%weight 4]}
3. d4 {The centre.} ( 3. Nf3!?
{Fine, and 3...Nf6 4.d3 is the kind of move the pawn on c3 was not for.} ) 3... Nf6
{The knight hits e4 with the wedge no longer available: e5 would be met by ...dxe5. [%weight 8]}
4. Bd3
{The one line where the bishop comes before the knight. It defends e4 and keeps Nf3 for next move, so that ...Bg4 has nothing to pin yet and ...cxd4 can be taken back with the pawn. [%tags development]}
( 4. e5?!
{4...dxe5 5.dxe5 Qxd1+ 6.Kxd1 Nd5 and the queens are off with White's king in the middle and the pawn on e5 loose.}
) ( 4. Nd2!?
{Sound, defending e4 with the knight, and it blocks the bishop that wants d3 or c4.} ) (
4. Nf3?! {4...Nxe4 and the pawn has gone: nothing was defending it.} ) 4... Nc6
{Black develops, hitting d4. [%weight 6]} 5. Nf3
{Now the knight, with d4 held by it and the queen. White has the full centre and every piece coming to a natural square. [%tags development]}
( 5. d5?!
{5...Ne5 and the knight has a square in front of the pawn that nothing takes it from.} ) (
5. Ne2!?
{Sound and slower, keeping f3 for the pawn. The knight on f3 is the one the position wants.}
) 5... Bg4 {The pin, now that there is a knight to pin. [%weight 6]} ( 5... cxd4
{Black takes. [%weight 5]} 6. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} ( 6. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and 6...Nxd4 7.cxd4 leaves one pawn in the centre where there were two. The pawn takes back and keeps both.}
) 6... Bg4 {The pin. [%weight 7]} 7. Be3
{The bishop holds d4 and the centre stands: two pawns abreast on d4 and e4 with every piece behind them. [%tags development] [%plan Nc3, castle, h3 to ask the bishop, and the centre pawns stay where they are until Black's pieces are placed; then d5 or e5, whichever hits more.] [%line 2...d6 and 5...cxd4, the full centre]}
( 7. d5!?
{Sound: 7...Ne5 8.Nxe5 dxe5 is the other line. With the c-file open the push gives Black's rook the file.}
) ( 7. Nbd2?!
{The knight blocks the bishop that wants e3, and the pawn on d4 is held by the queen alone; the bishop comes to e3 a move late.}
) ) 6. d5
{Now the push, hitting the knight, because the bishop on g4 has left b7 and the knight on c6 has one square, e5, where it can be taken.}
( 6. Be3!?
{Sound, and 6...cxd4 7.cxd4 e5 hits the centre with the pin holding f3. The push first, while the knight has nowhere good.}
) ( 6. O-O?!
{6...cxd4 7.cxd4 e6 and it is an isolated-pawn game with Black's pieces well placed, where the push had the knight on c6 short of squares.}
) 6... Ne5 {The knight goes to the only square that is not a retreat. [%weight 8]} 7. Nxe5
{Take. The knight leaves f3, so the bishop on g4 now looks straight at the queen on d1, and the queen has to move next; it moves with a hit on b7. [%tags tactics]}
( 7. Be2?!
{7...Nxf3+ 8.Bxf3 Bxf3 9.Qxf3 and Black has traded two pieces off the board in a position where White wanted them on it.}
) 7... dxe5
{Black takes back with the pawn, and the bishop on g4 is looking at the queen. [%weight 9]}
8. Qb3
{The queen off the file with a hit on b7 and d5 held. Castling first would lose the queen: the bishop on g4 takes it. [%tags tactics]}
( 8. O-O?
{8...Bxd1 and the queen has gone: the knight that shielded it left f3 on the last move.} )
( 8. Qc2!? {Sound, off the file and holding e4. b3 hits b7 as well.} ) ( 8. f3!?
{Sound, blocking the bishop with a pawn, and it weakens the king for nothing the queen move does not do better.}
) 8... Qc7 {The queen defends b7. [%weight 7]} 9. Bb5+
{The check, forcing the bishop back to d7 where it blocks the queen's defence of nothing and can be traded. [%tags tactics]}
( 9. Nd2!? {Sound and it comes next anyway: 9...g6 10.Nc4 and the same shape.} ) 9... Bd7
{Black blocks. [%weight 8]} 10. Bxd7+
{Trade, so that the knight takes back onto d7 and blocks Black's own queen, and the knight on b1 comes to d2 and c4 against a pawn on e5 that has only the queen to hold it. [%tags development] [%plan Nd2 and Nc4 against the pawn on e5, castle, and f4 to open the f-file when the knight is on c4. The pawn on d5 is a wedge in Black's camp, and the king on e8 has no good home.] [%line 2...d6, the queen off the file]}
( 10. Nd2!? {Sound and the same thing a move later: 10...g6 11.Nc4 Bg7 12.Bxd7+.} ) (
10. Qc4?! {10...Bxb5 11.Qxb5+ Qd7 and the queens come off with White's edge traded away.}
) ) ( 2... g6
{The fianchetto. Black will put the bishop on the long diagonal and hit d4 with it. [%weight 4]}
3. d4
{The centre, and against the fianchetto it is the full centre: both pawns stay where they are and the bishop on g7 looks at a wall.}
( 3. Nf3!? {Fine: 3...Bg7 4.d4 is the same. The pawn first.} ) 3... cxd4
{Black takes. [%weight 7]} 4. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} ( 4. Nf3!?
