{Black breaks the pin. [%weight 8]} 9. Nxd7
{Take with the knight, keeping the bishop on b5 for one more move. [%tags development]} (
9. Nxc6?! {9...Bxc6 10.Bxc6+ bxc6 and Black has the bishop pair and the file.} ) (
9. Bxc6!? {Fine, and it gives the bishop pair away when the knight can take instead.} )
9... Qxd7 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 10. dxc5
{Take the pawn. With the c-pawn kept on c5 for six moves, it is loose now: the queen on d7 does not reach it, and the knight on b6 has to spend a move going to d5 to get it back, if it can. [%tags tactics]}
( 10. O-O?!
{10...cxd4 11.cxd4 e6 and Black has taken on his own terms with everything defended.} ) (
10. a4!? {Sound, hitting the knight, and the pawn on c5 is there to be taken now.} ) (
10. Be3?!
{10...cxd4 11.Bxd4 Qd5 and the tension is gone on Black's terms, with the pawn on c5 that was free no longer there.}
) 10... Nd5
{The knight goes to d5, hitting c3 and looking at the pawn on c5 from a square the bishop on b5 cannot reach. [%weight 7]}
11. Nd2
{The knight to d2, on its way to b3 to hold the pawn on c5, with the bishop on b5 still pinning the knight on c6. [%tags development] [%plan Nb3 to hold c5, castle, and the pawn on c5 is a wedge in Black's camp with the d-file half open behind it. If Black takes on c3, take back with the b-pawn and the b-file is open too.] [%line 4...d6 with the c-pawn kept]}
( 11. Bxc6!?
{Sound: 11...Qxc6 12.Nd2 and the pawn on c5 is held by the knight next. The knight first keeps the bishop pair.}
) ( 11. O-O!?
{Fine: 11...Nxc3 12.bxc3 and the pawn on c5 is still there with the b-file open. The knight to d2 first keeps the structure.}
) *


[CourseId "alapin-sicilian"]
[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch5-everything-else"]
[ChapterTitle "Everything else"]
[DefaultTags "big-centre"]

{The moves that are left: the ones a club player plays once and never again, and the two queen moves that look like they are doing something. One safe recipe each. None of them needs more than the rule from chapter 3, play d4 and take back with the pawn, with one exception that the chapter makes a point of: 2...Qa5 pins the c-pawn to the king, so the pawn cannot take back until the king has moved.

## The moves

2...a6 is a waiting move and d4 comes at once. 2...Qc7 puts the queen on the c-file early and d4 asks what it is doing there. 2...Qa5 is the one with a point, the pin on c3, and the answer is to develop, hold e4 with the queen, castle, and only then play d4. 2...f5 gives up a pawn for nothing much and the recipe takes it and keeps it. And 2...Nc6 3.d4 with an early ...cxd4 and ...Qb6 is the try that hits b2 while the queen's knight is still at home; the pawn goes to d5 at once, hitting the knight, and the queen's raid on b2 runs into Bd4 and a check that ends on c3.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Against 2...Qa5, playing d4 and finding that cxd4 is illegal, so that the knight has to take back. Against ...Qb6, defending b2 rather than hitting the knight with d5, and worst of all playing Nc3, after which ...Qxb2 and ...Qxa1 take a rook. And against 2...f5, declining a free pawn to keep the structure: the pawn is the structure.}
1. e4 c5 2. c3 a6
{A waiting move, keeping b5 for later. It does nothing about the centre. [%weight 4]} (
2... Qc7
{The queen to c7 early, looking at e5 and the c-file. It does nothing yet. [%weight 3]}
3. d4 {The centre.} ( 3. Nf3!? {Fine. The pawn first.} ) 3... cxd4
{Black takes. [%weight 7]} 4. cxd4
{The pawn takes back, and the queen on c7 is on an open file with nothing on it.} (
4. Nf3?! {4...dxc3 5.Nxc3 is the Smith-Morra a move late.} ) 4... Nf6
{The knight hits e4. [%weight 7]} 5. Nc3
{The knight defends e4 and develops: with the c-pawn gone the square is free, and the queen on c7 is looking at a knight rather than a file. [%tags development]}
( 5. e5?!
{5...Nd5 and the knight is on its best square with the queen already hitting e5.} ) (
5. Bd3!?
{Sound, defending with the bishop. The knight does it and develops the harder piece.} )
5... d6 {Black holds e5 and opens the bishop. [%weight 7]} 6. Bd3
{Develop, pointing at h7. [%tags development]} ( 6. Nf3!?
{Fine: 6...Bg4 pins it. The bishop first keeps the knight's square for a moment when the pin has no target.}
) 6... e6 {Black covers d5 and prepares to develop. [%weight 7]} 7. Nf3
{Develop. [%tags development]} ( 7. Nge2!?
{Sound, keeping f3 for the pawn. The knight on f3 is the natural square.} ) 7... Be7
{Black develops. [%weight 8]} 8. O-O
{Castle. White has the full centre and every piece out; Black's queen has spent a move to sit on a file with a knight in front of it. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Qe2, Re1 and Bg5, with e5 when the knight on f6 has nowhere to go. The queen on c7 is hit by Nb5 whenever a6 has not been played.] [%line 2...Qc7, the queen out early]}
( 8. e5?!
{8...dxe5 9.dxe5 Nd5 and the wedge is a target rather than a threat, with the king still in the middle.}
) ) ( 2... Qa5
{The one second move with a point: the queen pins the pawn on c3 to the king, so that d4 cannot be answered by cxd4 until the king has moved. [%weight 2]}
3. Nf3
{Develop, and castle before d4. The pin is real for as long as the king is on e1, and it is nothing the moment the king is on g1. [%tags development]}
( 3. d4?!
{3...cxd4 and the c-pawn cannot take back: it is pinned to the king. 4.Nf3 dxc3 5.Nxc3 is a gambit, and 4.Qxd4 Nc6 hits the queen with the centre gone.}
) ( 3. Bc4!?
{Sound: 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 is the same. The knight first, because it has to come anyway.} )
3... Nc6 {Black develops. [%weight 7]} 4. Bc4
{The bishop to c4, and the king castles next. [%tags development]} ( 4. d4?!
{4...cxd4 and again the pawn cannot take back: 5.Nxd4 is the open Sicilian with a queen on a5 that is about to be hit by Nb3 or Bd2, which is fine for White but is not this course.}
) ( 4. Be2!? {Fine and quieter. c4 points at f7 and at the d5 square the queen will want.}
) 4... Nf6 {Black develops and hits e4. [%weight 7]} 5. Qe2
{The queen defends e4, which the knight is hitting, and it does so without moving the king: the pin on c3 is still there, and castling first would leave e4 to be taken. [%tags development]}
( 5. O-O?
{5...Nxe4 and the pawn has gone: nothing was defending it once the knight came to f6.} ) (
5. e5!?
{Sound: 5...d5 6.Be2 Nd7 7.d4 and the wedge is held. The queen move keeps the structure the course teaches.}
) ( 5. d3!?
{Sound, defending with the pawn, and the d-pawn wants to go two squares once the king has moved.}
) 5... d6 {Black holds e5 and opens the bishop. [%weight 7]} 6. O-O
{Castle. The king leaves e1, and with it the pin on c3 is gone: d4 comes next with the pawn able to take back. [%tags king-safety]}
( 6. d4?!
{6...cxd4 and the c-pawn is still pinned to the king: 7.Nxd4 is the open Sicilian the course was chosen to avoid.}
) ( 6. h3!? {Sound and slow. The king first, because it is the pin that has to go.} )
6... e6 {Black covers d5 and opens the bishop. [%weight 7]} 7. d4
{Now the centre, with the king on g1 and the c-pawn free to take back.} ( 7. Bb3!?
{Sound and quiet, taking the bishop off the file Black's pawn may open. The pawn move is the point of the opening.}
) ( 7. Re1!? {Sound and slow. The pawn now, because the pin that stopped it has gone.} )
7... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 7]} 8. cxd4
{The pawn takes back, and the queen on a5 is on the edge of a game that is happening in the centre. [%plan Nc3 with tempo on the queen, Bd2 when the queen wants a5 for a second time, and e5 against the knight on f6. The queen has to move again before Black has castled.] [%line 2...Qa5, the queen holds e4]}
( 8. Nxd4?!
{The knight takes and the centre is one pawn, where the pawn recapture keeps two and the queen on a5 keeps looking at nothing.}
) ) ( 2... f5 {A pawn offered for the f-file and nothing else. [%weight 2]} 3. exf5
{Take it. The pawn is free, and keeping it is the whole recipe. [%tags tactics]} ( 3. e5!?
{Sound, and it declines a free pawn to keep a structure. The pawn is the structure.} ) (
3. d3?! {3...fxe4 4.dxe4 and Black has traded off White's centre pawn for a wing pawn.} )
3... Nf6 {Black develops, meaning to take the pawn back later. [%weight 6]} 4. d4
{The centre, with the extra pawn on f5 kept for now.} ( 4. g4?!
{Holding the pawn with a pawn move in front of the king: 4...h5 and the kingside is open before anything is developed.}
) 4... d5 {Black hits the centre. [%weight 6]} 5. Bd3
{The bishop holds the pawn on f5 and develops. [%tags development]} ( 5. dxc5?!
{5...Bxf5 and Black has the pawn back with the bishop on its best square.} ) ( 5. Nf3!?
{Sound: 5...Bxf5 6.Bd3 and the pawn has been given back for a tempo. The bishop first keeps it.}
) 5... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 6]} 6. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} ( 6. Nf3!?
{Sound, and the engine's choice: 6...e5 7.fxe6 and the pawns come off into an attack. The course takes back with the pawn, which is simpler and keeps the centre.}
) 6... Nc6 {Black develops, hitting d4. [%weight 6]} 7. Nf3
{Develop, holding d4. White is a pawn up with the centre and every piece coming out; the f-file Black paid for is closed by his own knight. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, Nc3, and keep the pawn on f5 with Bd3 and g4 only when the king is safe. Give the pawn back only for the centre or for development, never for nothing.] [%line 2...f5, the pawn taken and kept]}
( 7. Ne2!? {Sound, keeping f3 for the pawn. The knight on f3 holds d4 and e5 both.} ) (
7. Bb5?!
{The bishop leaves the pawn on f5 it was holding: 7...Bd7 and the bishop has to decide with nothing gained.}
) ) ( 2... Nc6
{The knight, and this chapter takes the line where Black hits b2 with the queen. [%weight 4]}
3. d4 {The centre.} 3... cxd4 {Black takes at once. [%weight 7]} 4. cxd4
{The pawn takes back.} ( 4. Nf3?!
{4...dxc3 5.Nxc3 is the Smith-Morra, a gambit the course does not play.} ) 4... Qb6
{The queen hits b2 and d4 at once, while White's queen's knight is still at home. [%weight 4]}
5. d5
{The push, hitting the knight, and it answers the queen too: after the knight moves the bishop comes to e3 with tempo on b6, and the pawn on b2 is poison because Bd4 and a check that lands on c3 chase the queen round the board.}
( 5. Nc3?
{5...Qxb2 6.Nb5 Qxa1 and the rook has gone: with the knight off b1 nothing covers a1, and the fork on c7 is worth less than a rook.}
) ( 5. Nf3!?
{Sound, and 5...Qxb2 6.Nbd2 hands the pawn over for development. The push keeps the pawn and wins time.}
) ( 5. Bd3?! {5...Qxd4 and the pawn has gone with nothing for it.} ) 5... Ne5
{The knight goes forward, the only square that is not a retreat. [%weight 6]} ( 5... Nd4
{The knight jumps to d4, where it is hit at once. [%weight 4]} 6. Be3
{The bishop hits the queen, and the knight on d4 is short of squares: it has to go back or be traded on Black's terms. [%tags tactics] [%plan Nc3, Nf3 to trade the knight on d4 when it suits, and Bd3 and castle. The pawn on d5 is a wedge in Black's camp and the queen on b6 is a target for the bishop.] [%line 5...Nd4, the knight hit]}
( 6. Nc3!?
{Sound: 6...e5 7.Nf3 and the knight on d4 is asked to leave. The bishop asks the queen first.}
) ( 6. Nf3?!
{6...Nxf3+ 7.Qxf3 e5 and the knight has been traded before the bishop could hit the queen, with the tempo gone.}
) ) 6. Be3
{The bishop hits the queen and covers d4, and now b2 is a trap: Qxb2 walks into Bd4. [%tags tactics]}
( 6. Nf3?!
{6...Nxf3+ 7.Qxf3 Qxb2 and the pawn has gone with the rook on a1 loose behind it.} )
6... Qxb2 {Black takes the pawn. [%weight 5]} ( 6... Qa5+
{The queen checks its way back to safety rather than take the pawn. [%weight 5]} 7. Nc3
{Block with the knight, developing with tempo, and the knight on c3 covers b5 and d5 as well. [%tags development] [%plan f4 against the knight, Nf3, Bd3 and castle. White has the centre, the pieces and the tempi; Black has a queen on a5 and a knight looking for a square.] [%line ...Qa5+, the queen retreats]}
( 7. Nd2?!
{The knight blocks on the wrong square: it is in the bishop's way and it does not cover d5, and the pawn on d5 needs it.}
) ( 7. Qd2?!
{7...Qxd2+ 8.Nxd2 and the queens are off with White's development spent on a trade.} ) )
7. Bd4
{The bishop hits the queen and the knight at once. The queen has one check and then nothing. [%tags tactics]}
( 7. Nc3? {7...Qxc3+ 8.Bd2 and the knight has gone for nothing.} ) ( 7. Nd2?!
{7...Qb4 and the queen is back with the pawn, the bishop on e3 doing nothing.} ) 7... Qb4+
{The check, the queen's only good square. [%weight 8]} 8. Nc3
{Block with the knight, which comes out with tempo to the square it wanted. The queen is on b4 with nowhere to go, the knight on e5 is hit by the bishop, and Black is a pawn up in a position he would give two pawns to be out of. [%tags development] [%plan f4 to hit the knight, Rb1 to hit the queen, Nf3 and the king castles: every White move comes with a threat and Black is answering them with a queen that has moved five times.] [%line ...Qxb2, the queen hunted]}
( 8. Qd2?
{8...Qxd2+ 9.Nxd2 and the queens are off with the pawn gone and the hunt over: everything White had built comes to nothing.}
) ( 8. Ke2? {8...b6 9.Nc3 Ba6+ and the king is in the open with the queens on.} ) ) 3. d4
{The centre, at once.} ( 3. Nf3!?
{Fine, and 3...d5 4.e5 is a French shape. The pawn first.} ) ( 3. a4?!
{A pawn move to stop a pawn move, while the centre waits. 3...d5 and Black has the centre fight on his terms.}
) 3... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 7]} 4. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} ( 4. Nf3?!
{4...dxc3 5.Nxc3 is the Smith-Morra a move late, a gambit the course does not play.} )
4... d5 {Black hits the centre. [%weight 7]} 5. exd5
{Take, and it is chapter 2 with ...a6 in: a move Black did not need. [%tags isolated-pawn]}
( 5. e5!?
{Sound, the French shape with the pawn on a6 doing nothing. The trade keeps the course's structure.}
) ( 5. Nc3!? {Sound: 5...dxe4 6.d5 is chapter 3's push. The trade is simpler.} ) 5... Qxd5
{The queen takes back. [%weight 8]} 6. Nf3 {Develop. [%tags development]} ( 6. Nc3!?
{Fine: 6...Qd6 and the knight has come with tempo. The knight to f3 first covers e5 and d4 before the other knight arrives.}
) 6... Nc6 {Black develops. [%weight 7]} 7. Nc3
{The knight with tempo on the queen. [%tags development]} ( 7. Be3?!
{It lets the tempo go: 7...Bg4 and the knight on b1 comes out a move later than it could have.}
) ( 7. Bd3?!
{It lets the tempo go: 7...Bg4 and Black is developing while the knight on b1 still has to come out.}
) 7... Qd6 {The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 8. d5
{The push, because the knight on c6 has to move and the only square that is not a retreat is e5, where it is traded with check to follow: after 8...Ne5 9.Nxe5 Qxe5+ 10.Be2 Black's queen has moved four times and White's pieces are all coming out.}
( 8. Be2?!
{Sound development and it lets the moment go: 8...Nf6 9.O-O and it is a level isolated-pawn game, where the push was a pawn's worth better.}
) ( 8. Bc4?!
{8...Nf6 and the knight on c6 has a defender before the push arrives, so the push a move later is worth much less.}
) ( 8. g3?!
{A quiet move where the push hits the knight with check to follow; 8...Bg4 and the moment has gone.}
) 8... Ne5 {The knight goes forward. [%weight 8]} 9. Nxe5
{Take, so that the queen has to take back and walk into a check that develops a piece. [%tags tactics]}
( 9. Be2!?
{Sound: 9...Nxf3+ 10.Bxf3 and the same shape with the queens on. The trade first gains the tempo.}
) 9... Qxe5+ {The queen takes back with check. [%weight 9]} 10. Be2
{Block with the bishop, developing with tempo: the queen on e5 is in the centre with White's pieces about to hit it. [%tags development] [%plan Castle, Re1 hitting the queen, Bf4 and Qd2. The pawn on d5 is a wedge in Black's camp and the queen on e5 has to move again before Black has developed anything.] [%line 2...a6, the push to d5]}
( 10. Qe2?! {10...Qxe2+ 11.Bxe2 and the queens are off with White's edge traded away.} ) (
10. Ne2?
{10...Nf6 and the pawn on d5 is hit by the queen and the knight with only the queen holding it. The bishop blocks and keeps d5.}
) *


[CourseId "alapin-sicilian"]
[CourseTitle "The Alapin Sicilian"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "The pawn centre against the Sicilian, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "e5-wedge isolated-pawn big-centre d5-break queenside-space vs-nf6 vs-d5 vs-closed vs-qb6"]
[ChapterId "ch6-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{Six positions, each running through a line from an earlier chapter and stopping on the move that matters. Play the line and find the last move yourself. Four of them are White's plans against a Black setup that has settled; two are Black moves that have to be answered exactly, because the natural answer loses something.

## What each one is about

The first is the battery. In the isolated-pawn position with every piece placed, the bishop goes back to b1 so that the queen can come behind it to d3, and the pawn on d4 is suddenly the least of Black's problems.

The second is the knight to g5 with the wedge on e5. With Black's knight gone from f6 and a bishop on d3, Ng5 threatens the bishop on h7 with the queen to follow, and the only good answer gives up the bishop pair.

The third is the pin on b5. Against 2...e6 and 3...d5 the knight on c6 holds c5 and d4, and the bishop on b5 takes both jobs away from it.

The fourth is the d5 break against the pin line: the queen on b3 has made the rook go to b8, and the pawn on d5 opens the file the queen is on and the diagonal from e3 at the same time.

The fifth is a defence: ...Qb6 hitting b2 and d4 with the queen's knight at home, and the answer is the push d5, not a piece to cover b2. The line runs through the whole hunt.

The sixth is a defence too: ...Bg4 pinning the knight on f3 while the queen sits on d5, and the answer is the bishop to e2 and nothing else, because the bishop to e3 lets the queens off with the pawns wrecked.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Answering a threat with a piece when a pawn move answers it with tempo. Trading the bishop that points at the king because a knight is offered for it. Playing a break because the pawn can move rather than because a line opens onto something. And covering a pawn that is poison, when the poison is the point.}
1. e4 c5 2. c3 d5
{The isolated-pawn line, run on past chapter 2 in two directions. [%weight 5]} ( 2... Nf6
{The knight line, run to the position where the knight has left f6 for good. [%weight 5]}
3. e5 {The wedge.} 3... Nd5 {The knight to d5. [%weight 9]} 4. d4 {The centre.} 4... cxd4
{Black takes. [%weight 8]} 5. Nf3 {Develop first.} 5... e6
{Black covers the knight. [%weight 6]} 6. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} 6... b6
{The bishop is coming to b7. [%weight 6]} 7. Nc3 {The knight hits the knight.} 7... Nxc3
{Black trades. [%weight 8]} 8. bxc3 {The b-pawn takes back.} 8... Qc7
{The queen hits the wedge. [%weight 7]} 9. Bd2 {The bishop defends c3.} 9... Bb7
{The bishop to b7. [%weight 8]} 10. Bd3
{The bishop points at h7. Chapter 1's line ends here; the exercise goes on.} 10... Be7
{Black develops the other bishop. [%weight 8]} 11. O-O
{Castle, so that the rook can come to e1 behind the wedge and the king is out of the centre before the attack starts. [%tags king-safety]}
( 11. a4!?
{Sound, gaining space on the queenside; the king first, because the attack needs the rook on e1.}
) ( 11. h4!?
{Sound and early: the pawn is coming to h5 one day, and the king would rather be castled when it goes.}
) ( 11. Rb1!? {Fine, on the half-open file, and it is a move that can wait.} ) 11... O-O
{Black castles, into a kingside with no knight on f6 to defend it. [%weight 8]} 12. Ng5
{The knight to g5, threatening Bxh7+ with the queen to follow to h5, and Black's knight is not on f6 to stop any of it because the wedge took f6 away on move three. The only good answer gives up the bishop pair: ...Bxg5, and the bishop on d2 takes back onto the diagonal Black's king lives on. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan If Black takes on g5, take back with the bishop, meet ...h6 with Be7 hitting the rook, and the dark squares round Black's king are White's for the game. If Black plays ...g6, Qg4 and the queen joins with h4 to follow. If Black plays ...h6, Nh7 wins the exchange. And if Black plays ...Qc6 to cover, Bxh7+ and Be4 win the queen.] [%line Exercise 2: the knight to g5] [%cal Gf3g5,Bd3h7]}
( 12. Re1!?
{Sound, and the knight jump is stronger now than a move later: 12...d6 and Black's pieces start to arrive.}
) ( 12. h4!?
{Sound, the pawn on its way to help; the knight goes first and the pawn follows.} ) (
12. Rc1?!
{The rook on a file with nothing on it, while the kingside waits: 12...d6 and the moment has gone.}
) ) ( 2... e6
{The French player's move, and the third exercise is the pin that answers it. [%weight 4]}
3. d4 {The centre.} 3... d5 {Black hits the centre. [%weight 8]} 4. exd5 {Take.} 4... exd5
{Black takes back with the pawn. [%weight 8]} 5. Nf3 {Develop.} 5... Nc6
{Black develops, holding c5 and d4. White to play: the knight has two jobs and one square. [%weight 7]}
6. Bb5
{The pin. The knight on c6 holds d4 and c5; pinned to the king it holds neither, and dxc5 comes next with the bishop pair in hand and Black's d-pawn isolated. [%tags development] [%plan Chapter 3's line from here: 6...Bd6 7.dxc5 Bxc5 8.O-O Nge7 9.Nbd2 O-O 10.Nb3, with the pawn on d5 the target of the whole game.] [%line Exercise 3: the pin on b5] [%cal Gb5c6]}
) ( 2... Nc6
{The knight, and two exercises hang off it: the queen's raid on b2 and the pin on f3. [%weight 4]}
3. d4 {The centre.} 3... cxd4 {Black takes at once. [%weight 7]} ( 3... d5
{Black hits e4 with the knight already out. [%weight 6]} 4. exd5 {Take.} 4... Qxd5
{The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 5. Nf3
{Develop. Chapter 3's line ends here; the exercise goes on.} 5... Bg4
{The pin, with the queen on d5 behind the knight. White to play: the sixth exercise is the one move that answers it. [%weight 7]}
6. Be2
{The bishop to e2 and nothing else. It breaks the pin by developing, and it keeps the queens on: the bishop to e3 instead lets ...Bxf3 and ...Qxf3 trade the queens with White's pawns wrecked, and the knight to d2 blocks the bishop that wants d3 later. [%tags development] [%plan Chapter 2's pin line from here: ...e6 and h3 to ask the bishop, castle, Be3 to hold d4, the knight to c3 with tempo once the c-pawn has gone, and Qb3 against b7.] [%line Exercise 6: the pin answered]}
( 6. Be3?!
{6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Qxf3 8.gxf3 and the queens are off with White's pawns wrecked.} ) (
6. Nbd2!?
{Sound, unpinning by blocking, and the knight is better on c3 once the c-pawn has gone.} )
( 6. h3?! {6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Qxf3 8.gxf3 and the same wreck a move later.} ) ) 4. cxd4
{The pawn takes back.} 4... Qb6
{The queen hits b2 and d4 at once, with the queen's knight still at home. White to play: the fifth exercise is the answer, and it is not a piece to b2. [%weight 4]}
5. d5
{The push, hitting the knight. Chapter 5's line ends on the knight to c3; the exercise runs the hunt through.}
5... Ne5 {The knight goes forward. [%weight 6]} 6. Be3 {The bishop hits the queen.}
6... Qxb2 {Black takes the pawn. [%weight 5]} 7. Bd4 {The bishop hits queen and knight.}
7... Qb4+ {The check. [%weight 8]} 8. Nc3
{Block with the knight. Chapter 5's line ends here; the exercise goes on.} 8... d6
{Black gives the knight on e5 a pawn to stand behind. [%weight 6]} 9. f4
{The pawn hits the knight, which has to go back, and every White piece is coming out with a threat.}
( 9. Rb1!?
{Sound, hitting the queen first: 9...Qa5 10.f4 is the same. The pawn first sends the knight home.}
) ( 9. Bb5+!?
{Sound: 9...Nd7 10.Ne2 and the queen is still short of squares. The pawn is simpler.} ) (
9. a3!?
{Fine, chasing the queen to a5, and it spends a move on a queen that is going to be chased anyway.}
) 9... Nd7 {The knight goes back. [%weight 8]} 10. Nf3
{Develop, and Rb1 next hits the queen again. Black is a pawn up with a queen that has moved five times, a king in the middle and nothing developed; White has the centre, the pieces and the open b-file. [%tags development] [%plan Rb1 and Rb5 chasing the queen, Bd3 and castle, then e5 or f5 against a king that has not moved. Do not take on b7; the pawn is bait.] [%line Exercise 5: ...Qb6 met by d5]}
( 10. Rb1!?
{Sound: 10...Qa5 11.Nf3 is the same. The knight first, because it has to come anyway.} ) (
10. Be2!? {Fine, and the knight to f3 is the piece the attack needs.} ) ( 10. Qd2!?
{Sound and a little slow: 10...Qa5 and the queen has to be hit again.} ) ) 3. exd5 {Take.}
3... Qxd5 {The queen takes back. [%weight 9]} 4. d4 {The centre.} 4... Nf6
{Black develops. [%weight 8]} 5. Nf3 {Develop.} 5... e6 {The main line. [%weight 7]} (
5... Bg4 {The pin, and the fourth exercise runs the pin line to the break. [%weight 5]}
6. Be2 {Break the pin by developing.} 6... e6 {Black opens the bishop. [%weight 7]} 7. h3
{Ask the bishop.} 7... Bh5 {The bishop keeps the pin. [%weight 8]} 8. O-O {Castle.}
8... Nc6 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 9. Be3 {The bishop holds d4.} 9... cxd4
{Black takes. [%weight 7]} 10. cxd4 {The pawn takes back.} 10... Be7
{Black develops. [%weight 8]} 11. Nc3 {The knight with tempo.} 11... Qd6
{The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 12. Qb3
{The queen hits b7. Chapter 2's line ends here; the exercise goes on.} 12... Rb8
{The rook defends b7 and leaves the d-file, and the queen on d6 is the only piece on it. White to play: the pawn on d4 has been waiting for this. [%weight 6]}
13. d5
{The break. It opens the d-file onto the queen and the diagonal from e3 onto the rook's old square, and the pawn cannot be taken without something worse: after ...exd5 the knight on c3 takes back with the rook behind it, and after ...Nxd5 the queen on b3 takes on b7. [%tags d5-break] [%cal Gd4d5]}
( 13. Rfd1!?
{Sound, and the break is stronger with the rook already there: play it when the pawn can be taken back with the rook behind it.}
) ( 13. Rad1!?
{Fine, the same idea with the other rook. The break now is the point of the position.} )
13... exd5 {Black takes with the pawn. [%weight 8]} 14. Rfd1
{The rook to d1, behind the pawn Black has just taken, so that Nxd5 comes with the file behind it. [%tags development]}
( 14. Rad1!? {Fine, and the queen's rook is the one that wants c1 later.} ) ( 14. g4!?
{Sound, asking the bishop before taking on d5; the rook first keeps the threat simple.} )
( 14. Nb5?!
{14...Qd7 15.Nxa7 O-O and the knight has gone to the edge for a pawn while the centre waited.}
) 14... O-O {Black castles, giving the pawn back rather than defend it. [%weight 8]}
15. Nxd5
{Take with the knight, hitting the bishop on e7 and the queen's square, with the rook on d1 behind it. The pawn is back and every White piece is on an open line. [%plan Nf4 hitting the queen and the bishop on h5, Bc5 when the queen leaves the d-file, and the rook on d1 doubled with the other. Black's b-pawn is a target and his king has no dark-squared bishop to guard it.] [%line Exercise 4: d5 against the rook on b8]}
( 15. Rac1?!
{15...Rfd8 and Black has held the pawn a move longer with the file contested.} ) (
15. a4?! {15...a6 and the pawn on d5 is still there with nothing won.} ) ) 6. Be3
{The bishop holds d4 first.} 6... cxd4 {Black takes. [%weight 7]} 7. cxd4
{The pawn takes back: the isolated pawn.} 7... Nc6 {Black hits d4. [%weight 8]} 8. Nc3
{The knight with tempo.} 8... Qd6 {The queen to d6. [%weight 8]} 9. a3 {Take b4 away.}
9... Be7 {Black develops. [%weight 8]} 10. Bd3 {The bishop to d3.} 10... O-O
{Black castles. [%weight 9]} 11. O-O
{Castle. Chapter 2's line ends here; the exercise goes on.} 11... Bd7
{Black develops the last minor piece, to a square where it does little. [%weight 7]}
12. Qe2
{The queen comes to e2, off the d-file and ready to go to d3 behind a bishop on b1. [%tags development]}
( 12. Re1!? {Sound, and the rook is better on d1 or e1 once the queen has said which.} ) (
12. Nb5!?
{Sound, hitting the queen, and 12...Qb8 sends the knight back with nothing gained.} ) (
12. Qd2!?
{Fine, and d2 is in the way of the bishop on e3 going back when the battery wants it.} )
12... Rfe8 {Black puts a rook on the e-file. [%weight 7]} 13. Rad1
{The rook behind the pawn, so that d5 has a rook behind it when it comes. [%tags development]}
( 13. Rfe1!? {Sound, and the queen's rook is the one with nothing else to do.} ) (
13. Rac1!? {Fine, on the half-open file; the d-pawn wants a rook behind it more.} ) (
13. Rfd1!? {Fine, and it leaves the king's rook nothing to do on f1 or e1 later.} )
13... Rad8
{Black puts the other rook on the d-file, against the pawn. Every piece is placed. White to play: the pawn is defended twice and attacked twice, and the plan is not to defend it a third time. [%weight 8]}
14. Bb1
{The bishop goes back to b1, out of the knight's way and off the d-file, so that the queen can come to d3 behind it: the battery on the diagonal to h7. That is the plan the isolated pawn buys, and the pawn on d4 is the last thing Black should be looking at. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan Qd3 next, then Ne4 or Ng5 with the queen and bishop pointing at h7. If Black plays ...g6 to cover h7, the dark squares round the king are the target and Bh6 comes. b4 and a queenside push are the plan when the kingside holds.] [%line Exercise 1: the battery on b1]}
( 14. Rfe1!?
{Sound and slower: the rook is the piece that will follow the battery, not lead it.} ) (
14. Bc2!?
{The same idea a square higher, and c2 is where the queen wants to be. b1 leaves the queen the better square.}
) ( 14. b4!?
{The queenside expansion, sound and a different plan: it comes after the battery, not instead of it.}
) *
